---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Link from '../../components/Link.astro';
---

<Layout title="Editorial Policy — Motiva Hub" description="How Motiva Hub is written and checked: who writes, how sources are verified, how affiliate links stay separate from editorial judgement." image="/images/youssef-raihane.jpg" lang="en">
  <article class="article-body container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="card-meta">LEGAL</p>
        <h1>Editorial Policy</h1>
        <p class="card-date">Last updated: October 30, 2026</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="container">
      <h2>Who Writes This Site</h2>
      <p>Motiva Hub is written and edited by <Link href="/author/youssef-raihane/">Youssef Raihane</Link>. Every article carries the author's name and publication date; when an article changes materially, its updated date changes with it. There is no anonymous content and no ghostwritten section.</p>

      <h2>What We Publish</h2>
      <p>Essays and guides on habits, discipline, focus, books and physical practice. We favour fewer, longer pieces over volume: a piece is published when it says something we can stand behind, not to fill a calendar.</p>

      <h2>Sources and Claims</h2>
      <p>When an article makes a scientific or factual claim, the source is cited inline. Named studies are linked to the publisher or to their DOI record; book claims are attributed to the book and, where it matters, to the page or chapter. We distinguish three things clearly and never blend them: what a study shows, what a practitioner reports, and what we recommend.</p>
      <p>Where evidence is mixed or thin, the article says so. Where a finding comes from a small sample or a single lab, it is described as such. We do not present a mechanism as a guarantee.</p>

      <h2>Personal Testing</h2>
      <p>Several articles report a protocol we ran on ourselves. Those pieces state the duration, the exact protocol, the result, and the limits — including what the test cannot prove. A first-person experiment is evidence of one person's experience, not a clinical trial, and it is labelled that way.</p>

      <h2>Independence and Advertising</h2>
      <p>Advertising is served by Google AdSense and is visually separated from editorial content, with an "Advertisement" label. Advertisers have no say in what we cover, and no article is written for an advertiser.</p>
      <p>Some articles contain affiliate links (Amazon and similar programmes). The commission never changes the price you pay, and it never decides a recommendation: books we would not recommend are not linked. Every article containing affiliate links carries a disclosure at the top and a link to the full <Link href="/affiliate-disclosure/">Affiliate Disclosure</Link>.</p>

      <h2>Reviews</h2>
      <p>Book reviews state the edition reviewed, what the book does well, and where it falls short. A review is never paid for, and a book is never reviewed without being read.</p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>If you find an error — a misquoted study, a wrong number, a broken source — write to <a href="mailto:contact@the-motivahub.com">contact@the-motivahub.com</a>. Verified factual errors are corrected in the article, and the updated date is changed. Substantive corrections are noted in the piece itself rather than made silently. Attribution errors for photography are corrected the same way; see the <Link href="/credits/">photo credits</Link>.</p>

      <h2>What We Do Not Do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No paid placements presented as editorial.</li>
        <li>No fake testimonials, invented statistics or invented interviews.</li>
        <li>No medical advice: content is educational and does not replace a professional.</li>
        <li>No content that promises a guaranteed outcome.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>Editorial questions, corrections and partnership requests: <a href="mailto:contact@the-motivahub.com">contact@the-motivahub.com</a>. See also our <Link href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</Link> and <Link href="/terms/">Terms</Link>.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
